{"reply":"In Part I, Chapter 10, Helen Keller writes: “My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea.”

The change is not just that her disappointment fades: she comes to see taking the crab from its element as unkind and unwise, and finds comfort in the possibility that it returned to the sea.

There may be a gentle parallel with your sister’s flat: wanting to make things easier and wondering whether you’re imposing your own preferences can both be true. Perhaps the key is leaving her room to decide what suits her. What might change if you asked where she wants things before arranging them?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg2397-vb3cc1e13-sec012-ln1237-1254","source_location":"Part I, Chapter 10 — CHAPTER X, source lines 1237-1254","supported_claims":["In Part I, Chapter 10, Helen Keller writes: “My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea.”","The change is not just that her disappointment fades: she comes to see taking the crab from its element as unkind and unwise, and finds comfort in the possibility that it returned to the sea."],"exact_quote":"My disappointment was bitter at the time; but little by little I came to realize that it was not kind or wise to force this poor dumb creature out of his element, and after awhile I felt happy in the thought that perhaps he had returned to the sea."}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

